      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Yes, a portable power station can run an air conditioner if its inverter can handle the compressor's startup surge, which in practice means a unit rated for about 1,500W to 2,000W continuous or more for a small window AC. A 5,000 BTU window unit uses roughly 450W to 550W while the compressor runs, so a 2,000Wh power station keeps it going for about three to five hours depending on how often the compressor cycles. Larger window units and most portable ACs draw 800W to 1,400W and burn through a battery two or three times faster. An inverter-type AC, a small room and a solar array running during the day are what turn this from a short stopgap into something genuinely useful.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Can a Portable Power Station Really Run an AC?
          </h2>

          <p>
            It can, within limits that are easy to work out. Air conditioners are bigger loads than most of what people plug into a power station, but small window units are not out of reach. A 5,000 or 6,000 BTU window AC, the kind sized for a bedroom, typically draws around 450W to 600W while running. Many mid-size and large power stations can supply that continuously, and the bigger question becomes how long the battery lasts.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where people get caught out is the start. A conventional air conditioner compressor needs a short burst of extra current to get moving, often two to three times its running draw for a fraction of a second. If the power station's inverter cannot supply that burst, it trips its overload protection and shuts off, sometimes over and over as the AC tries to restart. A unit that could easily run the AC at 500W may still fail to start it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Central air conditioning is a different story. A typical central AC system runs on 240V and draws several thousand watts, and it is hardwired into the house. Most portable power stations output only 120V and cannot connect to it at all. A few large expandable systems offer 240V output and can be wired into a home panel through a transfer switch, but that is a whole-home backup installation, not plugging in a portable unit. For sizing a generator to central air, see our guide on <a href="/what-size-generator-runs-central-air-conditioning/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">what size generator runs central air</a>.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">The Numbers That Matter</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">450-550W</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">5,000 BTU window AC, running</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">2-3x</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical compressor startup surge</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">3-5 hrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Small AC on a 2,000Wh unit</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">800-1,400W</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical portable AC, running</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            So the realistic picture is this: a large power station can keep one bedroom cool for part of a night, or keep a small room tolerable through the hottest afternoon hours with solar helping. It cannot cool a whole house, and it will not run a big AC all night without a lot of battery. The sections below give you the numbers to see where your setup falls.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="watts" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How Many Watts Does an Air Conditioner Use?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The quickest estimate uses the efficiency rating. Running watts are roughly the cooling capacity in BTU per hour divided by the unit's EER, its energy efficiency ratio. Most window units have an EER of roughly 10 to 12, which means a 5,000 BTU unit draws around 420W to 500W, an 8,000 BTU unit around 670W to 800W, and a 12,000 BTU unit around 1,000W to 1,200W. The rated watts or amps on the unit's label or spec sheet give you the exact figure for your model.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">AC type and size</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical running watts</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical startup surge</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Notes</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">5,000-6,000 BTU window</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">450-600W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1,000-1,800W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Best fit for power stations</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">8,000 BTU window</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">650-800W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1,500-2,400W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Needs a large unit</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">10,000-12,000 BTU window</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">900-1,200W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2,000-3,600W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Short runtime even on big units</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">8,000-14,000 BTU portable</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">800-1,400W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1,800-3,500W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Less efficient than window units</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Inverter window AC</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Varies, low at part load</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Soft start, minimal</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Best match for battery power</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Portable air conditioners, the kind on wheels with an exhaust hose out the window, are the worst match for battery power. They are less efficient than window units of similar nominal size because the machine sits inside the room, and single-hose models pull room air out through the exhaust, drawing warm outside air in through gaps. Since 2017, US portable AC packaging has to show a DOE seasonally adjusted cooling capacity, which is usually much lower than the older ASHRAE figure printed next to it. Size by the lower number.
          </p>

          <p>
            Inverter air conditioners are the best match. Instead of switching a fixed-speed compressor fully on and fully off, they vary the compressor speed to match the load, so once the room reaches temperature they may settle at a fraction of their maximum draw. They also ramp up gently rather than slamming on, which largely removes the startup surge. If you are buying an AC with battery backup in mind, an inverter model is the single biggest improvement you can make.
          </p>

          <p>
            Remember the fan. On many units, fan-only mode draws well under 100W, so if the air outside has cooled in the evening, running the fan with the compressor off moves cooler air in at a tiny fraction of the cost. Some units also have an energy saver mode that shuts the fan off with the compressor, which reduces average draw a little more.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="surge" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Why Does the Power Station Shut Off When the AC Starts?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Almost always, it is the startup surge. A fixed-speed compressor motor starting from rest draws a large current for a fraction of a second, often listed on the nameplate as locked rotor amps. Power station inverters have two ratings: a continuous rating and a surge or peak rating, which is the brief overload they can tolerate. If the compressor's start demand exceeds the surge rating, or lasts longer than the inverter can hold it, the unit trips.
          </p>

          <p>
            The surge figures manufacturers publish are not all equal. One unit's peak rating may be held for a few milliseconds, another's for a few seconds, and that difference matters a great deal for compressors. Two units with the same headline numbers can behave differently with the same AC. The practical rule is to give yourself room: an inverter rated for at least two to three times the AC's running watts, or a continuous rating comfortably above its surge, is far more likely to start it reliably.
          </p>

          <p>
            The compressor also gets harder to start right after it shuts off. If the AC cycles off and you try to restart it within a minute or two, the refrigerant pressure has not equalized and the compressor faces a much heavier start. Most window units include a built-in delay to prevent this, but switching the AC off and immediately back on at the wall can defeat it. Give it a few minutes before restarting after a trip.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE FIX FOR HARD STARTS</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">A soft-start device can cut the surge substantially</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Aftermarket soft-start kits, widely used on RV air conditioners, ramp the compressor up more gradually and can lower the startup surge enough for a smaller inverter to cope. They are installed inside the AC, so follow the kit maker's instructions and be aware that opening the unit may affect its warranty. An inverter AC avoids the problem entirely.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            If your power station starts the AC sometimes but not always, try removing other loads before the compressor kicks on, keep the battery above about 20 percent, and let the unit cool if it has been working hard. Inverters deliver their surge ratings best when the battery and electronics are not already stressed. If none of that helps, the inverter is simply too small for that compressor.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="runtime" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How Long Will a Power Station Run an Air Conditioner?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with the same formula used for any AC appliance: usable hours equal battery capacity in watt-hours times about 0.85, divided by the average running watts. The 0.85 allows for inverter losses and a small reserve. The tricky part is the average. A compressor that runs all the time draws its full rated watts; one that cycles on and off to hold a temperature averages less.
          </p>

          <p>
            How much it cycles depends on how big the AC is relative to the room and how hot it is outside. A properly sized window unit in a bedroom on a warm evening might run its compressor half the time. The same unit on a 100-degree afternoon in a west-facing room may run continuously. For planning, assume continuous running during the heat of the day and roughly 50 to 70 percent of the time overnight, then treat anything better as a bonus.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Power station capacity</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">5,000 BTU (~500W)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">8,000 BTU (~750W)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">12,000 BTU (~1,100W)</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">1,000Wh</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~1.7 hrs continuous</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~1.1 hrs</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Inverter often too small</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">2,000Wh</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~3.4 hrs, 5-6 hrs cycling</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~2.3 hrs</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~1.5 hrs</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">3,600Wh</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~6 hrs, 9-10 hrs cycling</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~4 hrs</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~2.8 hrs</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">6,000Wh (expanded)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~10 hrs continuous, 15+ cycling</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~6.8 hrs</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~4.6 hrs</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Run an example. A 5,000 BTU unit drawing 500W on a 2,000Wh power station has about 1,700 usable watt-hours, so about 3.4 hours of continuous compressor time. If the compressor runs 60 percent of the time overnight, average draw falls to roughly 300W plus a little for the fan, and the same battery stretches toward five or six hours. That is enough to cool a bedroom before sleep and through part of the night, but not until morning.
          </p>

          <p>
            Thermostat setting has a large effect. Every degree you raise the set point shortens compressor run time, and cooling a room to 78 rather than 72 can make a meaningful difference in battery life. Pre-cool the room while you still have grid or solar power, close the curtains on sun-facing windows, and seal gaps around the window unit with the foam strips most come with. Each step reduces how hard the compressor has to work.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="capacity" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. What Size Power Station Do You Need for an AC?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Size the inverter first, then the battery. For a fixed-speed 5,000 BTU window unit, look for at least 1,500W continuous and a robust surge rating; 2,000W or more continuous is safer. For an 8,000 BTU unit, 2,000W to 2,400W continuous is a sensible floor, and for 12,000 BTU units and most portable ACs you are into the largest portable models with 3,000W or more. Inverter ACs can often run on smaller inverters because they do not surge.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then size the battery to the hours you want. Multiply the AC's average running watts by the hours you need, then divide by 0.85. Eight hours of a 5,000 BTU unit averaging 300W while cycling needs about 2,400Wh of usable energy, which points to a roughly 3,000Wh unit or an expandable system with an extra battery pack. Eight hours of an 8,000 BTU unit that rarely cycles off needs closer to 7,000Wh. Our <a href="/what-size-portable-power-station-do-i-need/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">power station sizing guide</a> covers the same method for every load in the house.
          </p>

          <p>
            Be honest about cost at those sizes. Units in the 3,000Wh to 6,000Wh class with large inverters typically cost well into four figures, and expansion batteries add more. That is reasonable if the system also covers your fridge, medical devices and internet during outages, but it is a lot to spend on cooling one room. For many households, an inverter generator in the right location is a cheaper way to run an AC for hours at a time; our comparison of a <a href="/portable-power-station-vs-generator/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">portable power station vs a generator</a> lays out the tradeoffs.
          </p>

          <p>
            Chemistry matters at this size too. Running an AC means deep, frequent discharges, and lithium iron phosphate batteries are rated for several thousand cycles compared with roughly 500 to 1,000 for older lithium-ion chemistries. If you expect to run an AC regularly, such as in an RV or a cabin, LiFePO4 is the better long-term choice. Our <a href="/lifepo4-vs-lithium-ion-power-stations/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">LiFePO4 vs lithium-ion</a> guide explains why.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="solar" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Can Solar Panels Run an Air Conditioner Through a Power Station?
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is where AC and solar line up better than almost any other pairing. The hottest hours are usually the sunniest ones, so the panels produce the most exactly when the compressor works hardest. With enough panel wattage feeding the power station during the day, the AC can run largely on solar while the battery covers the gaps from passing clouds and saves its charge for the evening.
          </p>

          <p>
            The math is panel watts times peak sun hours times a real-world factor of roughly 0.75. Peak sun hours in summer commonly run about five to seven across much of the US. A 400W array in good conditions might yield around 1.5kWh to 2kWh a day; an 800W array roughly double that. A 5,000 BTU AC averaging 400W over eight daytime hours uses about 3.2kWh, so 800W to 1,000W of panels is the territory where daytime cooling becomes mostly self-sustaining.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check the power station's solar input limit before buying panels. Many mid-size units accept 400W to 600W of solar, while larger models accept 1,000W to 2,400W or more, sometimes split across two inputs with specific voltage windows. Panels beyond the limit will not add power, and wiring panels outside the allowed voltage range can trip protection. Our guide to <a href="/how-to-charge-a-portable-power-station-with-solar/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">charging a power station with solar</a> walks through series and parallel wiring and input limits.
          </p>

          <p>
            Night is still the weak spot. Whatever the battery has stored at sunset is what the AC gets until morning, so a solar setup that keeps a room cool all afternoon may still leave you warm by 2 a.m. The fix is more battery, a smaller or inverter AC, a higher thermostat setting overnight, or switching to a fan once the outside air cools. For the time it takes the battery to refill, see our guide on <a href="/how-long-does-it-take-to-charge-a-portable-power-station/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how long a power station takes to charge</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="alternatives" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. What Are Cheaper Ways to Stay Cool on Battery Power?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Fans first. A box fan or pedestal fan typically draws 30W to 100W, and a DC fan often far less. On a 1,000Wh power station, a 50W fan runs for around 17 hours. A fan does not lower air temperature, but moving air across your skin makes a warm room feel several degrees cooler, and in many outages that is enough to sleep. Pair it with a damp cloth or a cool shower before bed for more relief.
          </p>

          <p>
            Evaporative coolers, sometimes called swamp coolers, use a fan blowing through a wet pad and draw roughly the same power as a fan. They genuinely cool the air, but only in dry climates. In humid regions they add moisture and make a room feel worse. If you live in the desert Southwest or the interior West, they are worth a look; elsewhere, skip them.
          </p>

          <p>
            Reduce the heat load. Close blinds and curtains on sun-facing windows during the day, open windows at night when the outside air is cooler, and avoid using the oven or other heat-producing appliances. Move sleeping to the lowest, shadiest room in the house, since basements and north-facing rooms often stay several degrees cooler without any power at all.
          </p>

          <p>
            Know when cooling becomes a health issue. The CDC warns that older adults, young children and people with certain medical conditions are especially vulnerable in extreme heat, and fans alone may not prevent heat illness when indoor temperatures climb into the 90s. If a long outage coincides with a heat wave, a public cooling center may be the safest option. Talk to your doctor if someone in the household has a condition that makes heat risky, and plan backup power around that need.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sizing only to running watts</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A power station that easily covers 500W of running draw may still fail to start a 500W AC. Compressors surge two to three times their running watts at startup. Size the inverter's continuous and surge ratings to the start, not just the run, or choose an inverter AC that soft-starts.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting the bigger BTU number</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Portable AC boxes often show two capacities: an older ASHRAE figure and a lower DOE seasonally adjusted one. The lower number reflects real performance. Buying by the higher figure leaves you with an undersized unit that runs constantly, which is the worst case for battery runtime.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Restarting the AC immediately after a trip</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Right after the compressor stops, refrigerant pressure makes restarting much harder, and a power station that tripped once will usually trip again. Wait several minutes before switching the AC back on, remove other loads, and let the inverter cool if it has been working hard.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Overbuying solar the unit cannot use</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Every power station has a maximum solar input, often 400W to 600W on mid-size units. Panels beyond that limit add nothing, and wiring outside the voltage window can trip protection. Check the input specification and match the array to it before buying panels for AC use.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting it to replace central air</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Central AC typically runs on 240V and draws thousands of watts through hardwired circuits. A portable power station plugged in by cord cannot run it. Cooling one room with a small window unit is realistic; cooling a house needs a whole-home system or generator installed by an electrician.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a 1,000Wh power station run a window AC?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only a small one, and not for long. If the inverter can start a 5,000 BTU unit, expect around 1.5 to 2 hours of continuous compressor time, a little longer if it cycles. Many 1,000Wh units have inverters of 1,000W to 1,800W, which may struggle with the startup surge of a fixed-speed compressor.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a power station run an RV air conditioner?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Large units can, but RV rooftop ACs are commonly 13,500 to 15,000 BTU and draw roughly 1,200W to 1,700W running, with a large startup surge. A soft-start kit often makes starting possible on a 2,000W to 3,000W inverter. Runtime is short without a large battery bank and substantial solar on the roof.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is an inverter AC better for a power station?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Inverter ACs ramp the compressor up gently instead of surging, and they slow down once the room is cool rather than cycling fully on and off. That means a smaller inverter can start them and the average draw is lower, which stretches battery runtime noticeably compared with a fixed-speed unit.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I run an AC on a power station while it charges from solar?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most units support pass-through, so solar input can feed the AC while the battery covers any shortfall. On a sunny day with enough panels, the battery may barely drop. The power station's maximum solar input sets the ceiling, so check it before adding panels for daytime cooling. Clouds will shift more of the load back onto the battery.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much battery do I need to run a small AC all night?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a 5,000 BTU window unit averaging about 300W while cycling, eight hours needs roughly 2,400Wh usable, so a 3,000Wh or larger unit. Hotter nights mean more compressor time and a bigger battery. Raising the thermostat a few degrees and pre-cooling the room on grid power helps considerably. An inverter AC lowers the requirement further.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does running an AC hurt a power station?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not if the load is within its rated output. Heavy draws make the unit run warm and loud, and every full discharge adds a cycle to the battery's count. LiFePO4 units are rated for thousands of cycles, so regular AC use is reasonable. Repeated overload trips are worth avoiding by sizing the inverter properly.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a power station run a mini-split?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Some 120V mini-splits draw a few hundred watts at part load and start gently, which suits power stations well. They are hardwired, though, so connecting one requires a transfer switch or a properly installed inlet. Have a licensed electrician handle that rather than improvising a plug-in connection. Many models need a dedicated circuit.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

